@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Unit-Testing Your Coordinators") {
        A coordinator is a plain `@MainActor @Observable` class, and every
        navigation call mutates its state synchronously. So the whole
        navigation layer is unit-testable: no host app, no rendered
        `NavigationStack`, no UI test, no view-inspection library.

        You will link the `ScaffoldingTesting` library, assert on pushes and
        policies, drive a presented sub-flow and its awaited result, cover a tab
        guard, and check a deep link and a restore round trip — all in
        milliseconds.

        @Image(source: "scaffolding-intro-banner", alt: "Scaffolding")
    }

    @Section(title: "Set Up the Test Target") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Add a unit-test target (**File > New > Target > Unit Testing
            Bundle**) and link `ScaffoldingTesting`, the package's second
            library. Everything else is plain Swift Testing.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Link the `ScaffoldingTesting` product into the **test target**
                — never into the app target, since it imports Swift Testing. It
                brings four helpers: `activated()`, `descendant(ofType:)`,
                `hierarchyContains(_:_:as:)`, and `waitUntil`.

                @Code(name: "Package.swift", file: "test-01-package.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Write the first suite. Mark it `@MainActor` — coordinators are
                MainActor-isolated — and start from `activated()`, which
                resolves the initial root the way the first render would.

                > `FlowStack`, `Root`, and `TabItems` resolve their destinations
                lazily. Skip `activated()` and pushes still work, but
                `topDestination`, `isRoot(_:)`, and `hierarchyContains` read as
                empty, because the root was never resolved.

                @Code(name: "PlanetsFlowTests.swift", file: "test-02-first-test.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Pushes, Policies, and Dismissal") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            `depth`, `topDestination`, `isInStack(_:)`, `count(of:)`, and
            `isPresentingModal` are the vocabulary for stack assertions. They
            are public, synchronous, and cheap.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Pin down the behaviours that are easy to break: the double-tap
                guard, an awaited push resuming exactly once when the screen
                leaves, and a seeded stack starting deep.

                > Assert through this API rather than reaching into
                `anyStack.destinations` — the framework's internals are not your
                contract.

                @Code(name: "PushPolicyTests.swift", file: "test-03-policies.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Modals and Results") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Presented coordinators are where tests usually stall: the child is
            created inside the framework, so how do you get at it? With the
            `expecting:` overloads, which navigate *and* return the resolved
            child, typed.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                When the **test** performs the navigation, `expecting:` hands
                back the child: prove the sub-flow owns its own stack, and that
                `dismissModal()` leaves pushes alone. `routeType` and
                `ancestor(ofType:)` confirm the child was wired to its
                presenter.

                @Code(name: "ModalTests.swift", file: "test-04-modals.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                When the **code under test** performs it, use
                `descendant(ofType:)`: `present(_:awaiting:)` deliberately hands
                back no coordinator, so this is how a test drives the presented
                flow and lets the awaited result come back. The second test
                covers the cancel path, which resumes with `nil`.

                @Code(name: "ResultTests.swift", file: "test-05-async.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Tabs, Roots, and Restoration") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The same approach scales to the top of the tree: tab guards are
            ordinary methods, root swaps are one call, and a whole-tree
            assertion is one string comparison.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Call `shouldSelect(tab:isReselection:)` the way the tab bar does
                and assert both the veto and its side effect. Remember the
                asymmetry: programmatic selection bypasses the hook, so a test
                calling `selectFirstTab` is testing selection, not the guard.

                @Code(name: "TabTests.swift", file: "test-06-tabs.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Cover the deep link end to end. `hierarchyContains(_:_:as:)`
                checks the whole tree in one typed assertion — no string
                matching, and it never materialises coordinators that do not
                exist yet, so the assertion cannot create what it is looking
                for.

                > Need more than yes/no? `hierarchySnapshot()` in the main
                library returns the same tree as `[HierarchyNode]` — role, meta,
                coordinator, children.

                @Code(name: "RootTests.swift", file: "test-07-deeplinks.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Finish with the restore round trip: navigate, capture, tear the
                tree down, replay, and compare.

                @Code(name: "RestorationTests.swift", file: "test-08-restoration.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                **Run the tests** (⌘U). The whole suite finishes in a fraction of
                a second, because none of it touches SwiftUI.

                Two things to leave untested: view rendering — that a
                `NavigationStack` shows the top destination is the framework's
                job — and `\.destination`, which reads as its default outside a
                real render, exactly as it does in a `#Preview`.
            }
        }
    }
}
